tion | ✅ no `[features]` table; verified under default & `--no-default-features` × debug & release |\n\n## What was built\n\n- `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md`, `CONFIGS.md` — Phase A artifacts, derived mechanically (I compiled a probe against the same glibc to enumerate exactly which inputs make `pow` set `errno`, rather than guessing).\n- `tests/common/mod.rs` — harness: `dlopen`s both `.so`s, calls only exported symbols, and compares **three** observables per call: return **bit pattern** (so `+0.0`/`-0.0` and NaN payloads are distinguished), caller-visible `errno`, and exact **stderr bytes**.\n- `tests/phase_b_configs.rs` (32 tests), `tests/phase_c_errors.rs` (27 tests), `verify.sh` (Phase D automation).\n\nRoughly 400k+ input pairs compared, including a 200k uniform-random-bit-pattern sweep and an exhaustive walk of all 2048 exponent fields × 4 mantissas × 2 signs for both operands.\n\n## Three findings worth flagging\n\n1. **Two \"failures\" were bugs in my tests, not the translation.** C and Rust agreed on both; I had mis-classified `(2.0, 1023.9999999)` as overflowing (it doesn't) and `(-2.0, 1023.9999999)` as a range error (it's EDOM — a negative base with non-integral exponent is a domain error regardless of magnitude). Reclassified rather than \"fixed\", per the rule that the C is ground truth.\n\n2. **19 tests initially failed for a harness defect, not a divergence.** `cargo test` runs tests on parallel threads, but fd 2 is process-wide, so concurrent captures stole each other's output. Return bits and `errno` matched everywhere — only `stderr` was scrambled. Fixed with a process-global lock over the capture's lifetime, plus routing all harness progress messages to stdout so they can never land in a capture file. Worth noting because this class of defect *looks* like a real divergence and could have driven a bogus \"fix\" to correct Rust code.\n\n3. **I added an anti-vacuity self-check**, since 59 green tests prove nothing if the comparison is broken. It runs two deliberately-wrong mutants through the same path: an always-`0.0` one (caught 6/6) and one using `f64::powf` (caught 5/6 — this is the *realistic* mistranslation, since `powf` never touches `errno` and would silently skip both error branches). It also asserts the random sweep actually reached all three `errno` states `{0, EDOM, ERANGE}`.\n\nOn the translation itself: the key correctness decision was already right — it calls glibc's `pow` via `#[link_name = \"pow\"]` and reads the same `__errno_location()` slot rather than using Rust's `f64::powf`. That is what makes the error branches reachable at all, and mutant B above demonstrates concretely what breaks without it.\n\n`c_src/` was not modified (source mtimes unchanged); the only addition there is the `build/` directory the task instructed me to create.","ttft_ms":2818,"type":"result","duration_ms":836716,"uuid":"eca4ce6a-d452-44cd-a3f1-8712cd173a35","ttft_stream_ms":2684,"time_to_request_ms":50,"first_content_frame_ms":2684,"queued_turn_count":0}
